ABSTRACT
The application of Additive Manufacturing (AM) within industrial settings brings new challenges to operational research. This paper provides in-depth analyses of nesting and scheduling problems in the context of AM. Using bibliometric and systematic review methods, we map the progression of knowledge in this territory and provide a detailed characterization of the existing mathematical models and approaches. Our findings indicate the fundamental decisions that predominate in these models and reveal the relationships between the critical studies, which form the backbone of the research. While the nesting and scheduling problem can be categorized into six types of decision, we have also found other emerging and interrelated optimization problems associated with nesting and scheduling decisions. To provide further details, an ample number of models are systematized in a reference framework for modeling nesting and scheduling in AM. Finally, pressing future research avenues that have emerged from our analysis and that need to be addressed to further advance the field are presented.

1. Introduction
Additive Manufacturing (AM) emerged out of rapid prototyping techniques and is often referred to as 3D printing (Attaran, 2017). In contrast to conventional subtractive and formative processing tech­ niques, AM deposits successive layers of material, which enables the production of highly complex parts and offers new degrees of flexibility for product design (Li et al., 2019). Nevertheless, the introduction of AM into manufacturing systems is still in its infancy, and existing mathe­ matical models still lag behind the practical needs of the new processes offered by AM. Therefore, at a time when consumers are calling for rapid responses, practical and efficient solutions rely on operational research and optimization models.
The scheduling problem is one of the most studied in operational research; nevertheless, AM brings additional challenges that have not received much attention until the last decade (Fera et al., 2020). This problem consists of grouping jobs into batches, and then schedule those batches over the available machines (Guzman et al., 2022) – referred as Batch Processing Machine (BPM) problem. AM machine scheduling is a variation of the pure BPM problem (Zhang et al., 2020). In this context, multiple parts, that correspond to jobs, are produced simultaneously in the same AM machine and during a single production cycle. Yet, in BPM

scheduling problems most of the existing research was conducted in the context of high-volume production and modeled as one-dimensional knapsack problems (Oh et al., 2020), which means that the machine capacity is constrained to a value, such as maximum weight, area, or volume. In AM scheduling problems, representing machine capacity involves modeling it as a nesting problem.
The nesting problem consists of packing multiple parts into a con­ strained build area or volume, which correspond to a batch in the BPM problem, for each production cycle (Alicastro et al., 2021). Typical de­ cisions include the location and orientation of the parts within the build – this introduces more complexity into the primary AM scheduling problem. In addition, the AM machine capacity in these problems can be modeled as a two-dimensional (2D) or three-dimensional (3D) binpacking problem. This is because certain processes, including Fused Deposition Modeling (FDM) and Stereolithography (SLA) require sup­ port structures, and it is not advisable to stack parts on top of each other. While other processes, such as Selective Laser Sintering (SLS), do not need support structures, and the machine capacity can be modeled as 3D. Therefore, the AM scheduling problem is characterized by inte­ grating nesting constraints into BPM scheduling problem.
Both scheduling and nesting problems have been described in a handful of noteworthy reviews in recent years. Dyckhoff (1990)

