introduced the typology of Cutting and Packing (C&P) problems, which is closely related to the nesting problem. The author suggested four classification criteria whereby C&P problems can be arranged: dimen­ sionality; kind of assignment; assortment of large objects; and assort­ ment of small items. Wa¨scher et al. (2007) proposed another typology, which is partially based on Dyckhoff’s original ideas. Araújo et al. (2019) focused on extending the existing typologies to the AM domain, proposing a classification according to four-tuples: dimensionality; optimization criteria; build volume type; and attributes of the assort­ ment of parts. This paper was instrumental in bringing together nesting and AM problems. For scheduling problems, Graves (1981) introduced a classification with three different criteria: requirements generation; processing complexity; and scheduling criteria. These criteria have been updated and outlined into systematic taxonomies (Kanet and Sridharan, 2000; Potts and Kovalyov, 2000; Slotnick, 2011; Perez-Gonzalez and Framinan, 2014).
Recent reviews have started to integrate nesting and scheduling problems, departing from the traditional perspective of separately addressing these decisions. Oh et al. (2020) introduced a noteworthy taxonomy based on three entities: Part; Build; and Machine. Herein, the interaction from Part to Build and Build to Machine imply nesting and scheduling problems, respectively. Nevertheless, this classification is restricted to the root of the nesting and scheduling problem and does not delve into the immediate problems of AM, including part decomposi­ tion, part distribution, and pre- and post-processing operations. In addition, the authors did not extend the study to new AM scenarios, such as collaborative manufacturing and multi-robot manufacturing. Our study therefore provides an up-to-date review of how these new con­ cerns are included within the nesting and scheduling problem.
De Anto´n et al. (2022) focused at the subproblem level. This level refers to breaking down the original nesting and scheduling problem into planning subproblems. Based on the hierarchy consisting of Part, Build, and Machine, they propose four planning stages to arrange the subproblems: Part; Part-Machine; Part-Build; and Build-Machine. The definitions of these subproblems were based on the general subproblems of Wa¨scher et al. (2007) and Maravelias and Sung (2009) and adapted to the AM context. While these authors focused on the subproblems at the operational level, we also study the integration of the operational with tactical level in AM.
Finally, Framinan et al. (2023) provide a literature review on the use of operations research methods in AM. The authors categorize AM de­ cision problems based on three business functions: Designing the AM process; Planning in an AM process; and Operating of AM. Nevertheless, the decision problems were addressed separately, and there is no informa­ tion of how these decisions are interrelated. This highlights the need for a review to consolidate the research on the integration of these AM decision problems, and to provide a coordination scheme for the different decisions.
Our review differs from the existing literature by specifically focusing on the incorporation of various decisions and constraints into the nesting and scheduling problem. Considering that these decisions are traditionally kept separate or loosely integrated, studying these in­ terdependencies aims at contributing to the development of new models and streamline the decision-making process. To study the progression of the knowledge in this field, we present an analysis of the critical papers, which form the backbone of the research, and utilize a scheme according to the three recognized entities in AM: Part, Build and Machine. Furthermore, this study applies a more comprehensive procedure than a traditional review, by using science mapping techniques to capture and map the existing knowledge on nesting and scheduling problems in AM. Two research questions are answered:
RQ1. What is the research structure of nesting and scheduling problems in the context of AM?
RQ2. What are the characteristics prevalent in the modeling of nesting and scheduling problems in AM?
