The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 out­ lines the methodology used. Section 3 presents the conceptual analysis and main path analysis. In Section 4, the characteristics of the existing mathematical models and approaches are analyzed, and the results systematized in a framework prior to Section 5, which outlines our future research program. Finally, Section 6 concludes.
2. Methodological approach
This paper follows the science mapping methodology proposed by Zupic and Cˇater (2015), which is comprised of five steps: research planning, data collection, data analysis, results visualization, and interpretation. All five are illustrated in Fig. 1. Web of Science (WoS) and Scopus were used for sourcing because they are widely used bibliographic databases. A set of keywords that comprehensively rep­ resents the sphere of interest was selected and combined into the following search string: (“additive manufactur*” OR “3D print*” OR “rapid prototyp*”) AND (“production planning” OR “scheduling” OR “nesting” OR “packing”). The initial search was restricted to Englishlanguage peer-reviewed papers. For this initial search on December 18 of 2023 we retrieved a total of 963 papers from WoS and 1,324 papers from Scopus. Next, title and abstract were screened to confirm the relevance of the papers and duplicates were excluded. This reduced our sample to 143 papers. In a second screening process, where the full texts were read, some further papers were discarded. Our final sample con­ sists of 130 papers: 105 journal articles and 25 conference proceedings. Finally, 7 articles were added through the snowball method.
The sample was subsequently exported to bibliometric software for further analysis. In this paper, the bibliometric analysis was primary conducted using Bibliometrix software, which is programmed in R lan­ guage for statistical analyses (Aria and Cuccurullo, 2017), more specif­ ically using the Biblioshiny interface. Pajek software, which has been extensively used to analyze network data, was also used to obtain the main path (De Nooy et al., 2018). Review papers were solely included for sample statistics, and for the conceptual and main path analysis. They were obviously excluded from the study on the characteristics of the models.
