3.1. Conceptual analysis
The conceptual analysis allows for a comprehensive exploration of the actual content of the papers to design the research structure of the field. Here, we focused on the decision types related to both nesting and scheduling problems to arrange the literature. Decision types refer, in this context, to the splitting of the main problem into specific decisions. Through these individual decisions, we gain valuable insights into the subtle complexities of the decision-making process within nesting and scheduling modeling. This directed the analysis towards six decision types, originating four from the nesting and two from the scheduling problem. The decisions are represented in Fig. 5 and associated to the entities: Part; Build and Machine. The entity Part is defined as the input of the nesting problem, and the resulting outcome is established by the entity Build. In the scheduling problem, the Build takes on the role of the input, and the Machine, with its schedule, is the output.
3.1.1. Decision types The first decision type is referred to as Build formation. Although
there are significant differences in processing techniques between traditional manufacturing processes and AM, it is noteworthy that the batch in BPM problems and the build in AM problems share similarities, albeit with some necessary adaptations. In AM, multiple parts may be grouped based on similar characteristics and produced simultaneously in the same machine (De Anto´n et al., 2022). The decision of grouping parts that share common characteristics, such as identical heights (Zhang et al., 2020; Yau and Hsu, 2022) or materials, directly impacts the production time and cost (Yau and Hsu, 2022).
In traditional manufacturing, build formation decisions have been usually modeled as one-dimensional knapsack problem (Oh et al., 2020), which means that the batch is reduced to a value, such as maximum weight, area, or volume. In AM, however, the machine

