capacity can be modeled as a 2D or 3D bin-packing problem. FDM and SLA are two technologies that require support structures. These struc­ tures are created simultaneously and from the same material as the parts. They serve to prevent part drifting and deformations. If a part is placed over another the support structure of the top part will interfere with the geometry of the top bottom part, at its top surfaces. This interference can significantly degrade the surface and possibly the dimensional accuracy of the bottom part, as well as increase the required post-processing time (Canellidis et al., 2013). For this reason, in FDM and SLA, parts are usually placed alongside each other; hence the binpacking problem essentially becomes 2D. Other technologies, such as SLS, do not need support structures, and the machine capacity can be modeled as 3D.
The second type of decision is the Machine selection. Each build is matched with AM machines whose dimensions are larger than the di­ mensions of the build. A major concern is the machine performance for producing a specific build (De Anto´n et al., 2022). Therefore, a set of efficiency criteria can be considered to select machines to builds. For example, one might choose the machine that offers the highest speed among the set of options or opting for the machine that provides the most robust quality assurances (Canellidis et al., 2013). Calabrese et al. (2022) addressed this problem by providing a decision-making frame­ work for choosing feasible machines according to production quality criteria. Maranha et al. (2023) provide a classification and decisionsupport framework for selecting AM technologies based on machine characteristics, process features, physical properties of parts, and cost.
In some studies the nesting problem is reduced to these two decision types (Kucukkoc, 2019; Yılmaz, 2020; Griffiths et al., 2019; Dickinson and Knopf, 1998). Authors utilize machine capacity constraints to ensure that the dimensions of the build do not exceed the dimensions of the machine. Nevertheless, to what extent can we claim that they have adequately addressed the nesting problem? All parts must be placed on the build so that they are fully contained within it and do not overlap. Hence, due to the modeling of overlapping needs among parts, issues related to the placement and orientation of the parts have the most substantial influence on the nesting problem (Griffiths et al., 2019).
Part placement responds to where parts can be positioned within the build boundaries, while ensuring there is no overlap between them. Dickinson and Knopf (1998) separate this decision type into parallel and serial placement. In the parallel placement, all parts are placed at once, whereas in the serial placement a rule will determine where the next part will be placed. Part orientation is often referred to as one of the most critical decisions within the nesting problem (De Anto´n et al., 2020). It specifies the best orientation policies that optimize the AM machine utilization, production time, cost, and quality of each build. Hence, each part can be rotated around X-, Y-, and Z-axes to determine the build orientation.
Once all the parts are placed and oriented in the builds, the nesting

problem is concluded, and we move on to the scheduling problem. This problem can be distinguished into two main decision types: Machine allocation and Sequencing. In machine allocation, the availability of the machines is checked so that each build can be assigned to one machine (De Anto´n et al., 2022). One machine can be assigned to several builds (single machine configuration). However, multiple machines in parallel are able to reduce production times and increase AM’s competitiveness over the traditional manufacturing processes (Kim and Kim, 2022). Indeed, there are two possibilities of AM parallel configurations: iden­ tical parallel and non-identical parallel machines. The latter can be characterized as heterogeneous machines, where each has its specifi­ cations, like different cycle times, setup times, and capacity constraints (Che et al., 2021). Some studies have addressed pre- and post-processing operations, which can be incorporated into flow-shop and job-shop settings.
In practice, pre- and post-processing stations can significantly differ from process to process. For example, Power Bed Fusion (PBF) tech­ nology require long cool-down times before the parts are removed (Maranha et al., 2023; Makanda et al., 2022).While in Vat photo­ polymerization, it is possible to remove the parts almost as soon as it is finished (Makanda et al., 2022). Hence, pre- and post-processing oper­ ations can significantly impact the production time. In this context, Kapadia et al. (2022) and Rossi and Lanzetta (2020) tackled flow-shop and job-shop systems, respectively. Aloui and Hadj-Hamou (2021) consider both pre- and post-processing times. Arık (2022) includes downstream post-processing times by considering assembly operations. Thürer et al. (2021) proposed workload control to provide a control solution for the post-processing shop.
Finally, the sequence of builds on each AM machine has to be determined. This is referred to as Sequencing. The sequencing plan specifies the order in which builds are to be processed in the machines, so that they meet certain production constraints, including capacity, production levels, precedence, start and due dates, machines capabil­ ities, machine availabilities, build restrictions, machine requirements, and machine availabilities. The sequencing plan has a huge impact on the performance measures of the scheduling problems.
3.1.2. Interdependent problems Interdependent problems, in this context, refers to adjacent optimi­
zation problems that have been integrated with the nesting and sched­ uling problem. This section aims at extending the analysis beyond the above-mentioned decision types and study a wider spectrum of inter­ related problems, that have the potential to affect or be affected by nesting and scheduling problems. For the sake of completeness, the existing literature is arranged into operational and tactical planning levels. By distinguishing the operational and tactical levels, researchers can integrate various insights from different planning functions and timescales. This steered the analysis towards two problems at the

