operational level: Part decomposition and Order acceptance; and one problem from the tactical level: Part distribution. Fig. 6 illustrates the interdependent problems found within the literature of nesting and scheduling in AM.
At the operational level, part decomposition widens the research scope to a precedent nesting-related problem. This problem can be used to split a larger part that in its current form exceeds the dimensions of the build space into multiple smaller parts (Oh et al., 2018); it can also be referred to as the printability problem (Yao et al., 2015). Part decomposition is also used to maximize productivity (production time, cost, and part quality); mechanical functionality; artistry; interchange­ ability; and to avoid the need for support structures (Thürer et al., 2021; Yao et al., 2015).
Several part decomposition methods have been developed in the literature. For example, Luo et al. (2012) suggested a framework to decompose a larger object into smaller dependent parts (sub-assemblies of the original object), known as Chopper. In their study, the focus was only on part decomposition, with no consideration of nesting concerns. Yet, some studies have addressed both decomposition and nesting challenges. For example, Chen (2015) proposed a decompose-and-pack (Dapper) algorithm to decompose an object into minor parts which can be efficiently packed. The Dapper algorithm aims to minimize support material, build time, and the assembly cost. Yao et al. (2015) developed a partitioning-and-packing algorithm, in which the partitioning compo­ nent is based on a level set representation. Demir et al. (2018) intro­ duced a divide-and-conquer approach, wherein the parts are derived through a near-convex decomposition. For a detailed and complete understanding of part decomposition, refer to the review of Oh et al. (2018).
Order acceptance can be related to the scheduling problem and occurs whenever the demand exceeds the available capacity of the resources, requiring the rejection of some orders (Kapadia et al., 2022). Hence, determining the set of accepted orders is of great importance within the broader decision problem. Nevertheless, in order acceptance, there exist two distinct scenarios. In the first and most prevalent scenario, the set of orders from different customers is known a priori (Kapadia et al., 2022). In the second scenario, referred to as dynamic, not all orders are known at the beginning of the decision period (Li et al., 2019). For example, Li et al. (2019) consider the order acceptance and scheduling problem with dynamic orders arrival where a part is considered accepted if it can be produced within one of the machines and completed within the delivery time requested by the customer. Kapadia et al. (2022) proposed an order acceptance and scheduling model considering placement and orienta­ tion issues, and post-processing requirements. Tafakkori et al. (2022) suggested a framework that considers negotiating with customers to change their desired due dates and assess the influence on the order acceptance and scheduling. For a thorough and comprehensive under­ standing of order acceptance and scheduling, refer to the taxonomy and review of Slotnick (2011).
Lastly, at the tactical level, scheduling issues have been integrated with the Part distribution problem. In this context, a set of orders has to be

