manufactured and transported by vehicles to customers (Yılmaz, 2020). While scheduling is vital for achieving efficient production, aligning it with distribution planning enables the minimization of transportation and inventory costs (Li et al., 2022). Solutions to this problem are characterized by production schedules that indicate the specific date, time and manufacturing location for customers’ orders as well as the distribution plan that satisfies constraints related to the availability of delivery vehicles, their capacities, and customer due dates (He et al., 2022). Although the integration of production and distribution planning has a considerable impact on supply chain performance, we found that little research attention has been paid to investigate such problems so far. Yilmaz (2020) were among the forerunners in exploring the inte­ gration of production and distribution aspects of the AM supply chain. Li et al. (2022) studied the production and distribution problem of spare parts considering two types of customers based on the urgency of de­ livery times. The authors suggest two different transportation policies to address the delivery time requirements of the two customers. He et al. (2022) integrate the production and distribution problem of spare parts with Just-In-Time (JIT) delivery times.
Overall, as nesting and scheduling problems have grown in complexity, additional issues have surfaced, unavoidably expanding the scope of their integration with other decision problems. Still, we note that the extent of integration remains somewhat limited. While the operational level has been extended to encompass two concerns related to nesting and scheduling (part decomposition and order acceptance), the introduction of AM processes in industrial settings poses new chal­ lenges for the operational planning.
3.2. Main path analysis: Nesting and scheduling research backbone
This section establishes the connections between the critical papers in an attempt to represent the backbone of the field (De Nooy et al., 2018). The main path analysis provides sound information about the relative influence of specific publications (Zupic and Cˇater, 2015). It is also examined over time, which is helpful for detecting research tradi­ tions and for leveraging prospective analyses (Aria and Cuccurullo, 2017). The combination of conceptual and main path methods provides an appropriate tool to fully understand the research structure of nesting and scheduling problems in the context of AM.
Citations indicate to some degree the scientific relevance of papers as well as their authors and journals. Citation network analysis can consequently be seen as an itinerary carrying scientific knowledge. The main path analysis is a unique method for citation analysis that explic­ itly focuses on the scientific progress of the field over time and illustrates the evolution of research (De Nooy et al., 2018). For this, each particular citation is given a traversal weight that indicates the extent to which a citation is needed for connecting papers. The search path count method was utilized to quantify the traversal weights and extract the paths. The path with the highest traversal weight is the main path. Fig. 7 presents the main path obtained using the Pajek key-route algorithm (De Nooy et al., 2018), enriched by qualitative analysis of seminal studies in the

