3.2.1. Seminal studies Ikonen et al. (1997) is a seminal paper of the main path. The authors
develop a Genetic Algorithm to solve a 3D nesting problem, in which parts are represented as non-convex geometries, including holes and cavities where other smaller parts can be placed. This makes the re­ lations between those parts very complicated. Intuitively, the authors place large parts first and fill the remaining space with smaller ones (this falls under the category of the serial placement problem). A second decision was the orientation of each part in the SLS machine. Each part had 24 allowed orientations (45 degrees of increment around each co­ ordinate axis) with finite relative positions constrained by five attach­ ment points, which define the points where the next part can be placed.
Li et al. (2017) is a seminal paper that introduced the problem of planning and scheduling in AM. The authors considered an objective

function based on the production cost per unit volume of material on non-identical SLM machines. The optimum allocation of parts into such AM machines was solved by a MIP model, and two heuristic procedures best fit and adapted best fit – are developed. Meanwhile, Chergui et al. (2018) were the first to integrate the planning and scheduling problem in AM with nesting concerns. In this work, a series of order due dates were considered and allocated to identical parallel machines. In contrast to the studies that preceded it, in this model the time spent on the AM machines is expressed in terms of part’s total production area and ma­ terial volume. The objective of the study is to minimize the tardiness and maximize the utilization of the machines. To solve the proposed model, the authors developed a heuristic based on the EDD rule.
3.2.2. Nesting problem Following the paper of Ikonen et al. (1997), Hur et al. (2001) applied
a modified Bottom-Left approach. This is a placement rule that implies positioning parts as close as possible to the bottom left-hand corner of the machine workspace (Araújo et al., 2020). This approach was implemented by a Genetic Algorithm. Subsequently, the orientation of the parts is optimized by considering their heights to achieve the min­ imum build height, reducing the production time and improving the surface quality. The authors contend that adopting the minimum build height as the objective function for problems involving dependent parts, resulting from the division of a larger part (part decomposition prob­ lem), is the most fitting choice, given the rough surfaces that emerge from the part decomposition process.
While the previous studies of the path have focused on the 3D nesting problem, Canellidis et al. (2006) proposed a 2D problem applied to SLA. In comparison to the SLS process, SLA requires support structures, and therefore, nesting solutions where parts are placed on top of other ones are unfeasible. The use of support structures increases the build time, as this includes the time needed to actually produce the parts and the time required for support removal, and surface finishing. In addition, the authors do not concern the actual geometries of the parts (irregular geometries) but rather minimum bounding boxes, making the problem more manageable and computationally less burdensome. In order to minimize the overall build time, a Left-Border-Down-Border rule was

