applied by a Genetic Algorithm for a serial placement solution. Later, the authors extended their method (Canellidis et al., 2013) by approxi­ mating the parts geometry through polygons instead of the common bounding box. A No-Fit-Polygon is employed as an additional placing rule based on the former Left-Border-Down-Border.
Gogate and Pande (2008) proposed a 3D nesting problem in which the part orientation problem is optimized based on the user criteria. This is operationalized by giving arbitrarily weights to each of the evaluation criteria in the objective function, including build height, average stair­ case error, volume of support structures, and contact interface of parts and support structures. By assigning different weights to interpolate between the different evaluation criteria, the problem can be custom­ ized to meet the functional requirements of several AM machines. Accordingly, the Bottom-Left method is implemented by a Genetic Al­ gorithm. Parts are represented through voxel models and could be rotated around the build axis with an increment of 45 degrees.
Zhang et al. (2016) addressed the 2D problem to solve the nesting problem through a two-step algorithm: first a AM feature-based orien­ tation involves determining the optimal build orientation for each part to ensure high production quality, and second a parallel optimization model is used to maximize the compactness of the nesting problem through the use of polygons to represent the geometry of the parts. To solve this problem, a Genetic Algorithm was used, which reinforces the idea that this metaheuristic is a competitive solution technique for solving nesting problems.
Nesting problems were the first AM decisions tackled by researchers, which is partly explained by the primary need to build multiple proto­ type parts simultaneously on a single building platform (Hur et al., 2001). As AM matured, there was an increasing need to be able to not only manufacture prototypes to facilitate rapid product development but also directly manufacture functional or end-use parts for specific applications (Zhang and Bernard, 2014). Owing to its advantage of manufacturing complex parts (surpassing traditional processes), AM has played a significant role in customized production. For all these reasons, nesting problems are critical to the AM planning decision-making.
3.2.3. Scheduling problem In comparison to the nesting problem, the focus here is not on
achieving the optimal placement and orientation of multiple parts on a build but rather on finding the best allocation and sequencing of builds in the AM machines (Oh et al., 2020). Li et al. (2017) paved the way for this problem (see section 3.2.1), leading to the subsequent studies indicated in our main path. For example, Ransikarbum et al. (2017), who consider a set of non-identical parallel FDM machines, and used a Mixed-Integer Linear Programming (MILP) model to assign a build per machine with the objectives of minimizing cost, maximizing load bal­ ance among machines, minimizing the total tardiness and minimizing the total number of unprinted builds.
Fera et al. (2018) addressed the single-machine scheduling; a set of builds were sequenced in a SLM machine, and a multi-objective model was formulated to minimize the production cost and the weight of the total earliness/tardiness. In their approach, the schedule that meets due dates with the least cost is solved using a modified Genetic Algorithm.
Meanwhile, Kucukkoc (2019) provides a stepping-stone to model advances for AM scheduling, since the authors addressed three different SLM configurations: single machine, parallel identical machines, and non-identical parallel machines. The different configurations were tackled through three MILP models to minimize the makespan, and the optimum solutions have been reported. The results showed limited applicability when problem size increases, which indicates the need for effective heuristics and metaheuristics to solve large problem instances.
The main path analysis also reveals that, at some point, the literature on nesting and scheduling is branched into two research streams. One stream is dominated by Fera et al. (2020), which represents the most evident research trajectory when compared to the previous papers. The other stream is led by the paper of Yılmaz (2020), which explores the

Computers and Operations Research 165 (2024) 106592
scheduling together with other supply chain functions. Later on, Oh et al. (2020) integrated both research streams in a comprehensive re­ view on nesting and scheduling problems in AM.
Fera et al. (2020) developed an improved Tabu Search to solve the same problem proposed in Fera et al. (2018). They showed that their algorithm outperforms the earlier modified Genetic Algorithm in solving small to large size instances. Yılmaz (2020) took the pioneering approach of investigating the combination of production and distribu­ tion functions in an AM supply chain to propose a two-stage scheduling problem, in which parts are produced on SLM machines by one manu­ facturer in the production step and then distributed to various customers by means of solving a transportation problem. A heuristic procedure with five selection rules was proposed to minimize the makespan, and computational experiments were conducted using problem instances generated for small and large size problems.
The need for solving AM scheduling problems results from an increasing adoption of AM machines in real manufacturing contexts (Fera et al., 2020). This shift asked for innovative models and methods capable of efficiently dealing with AM technologies. While the nesting problem looked at the AM problem in the realm of a single machine, this second problem comes as a step forward in bringing the AM planning closer to practical scenarios. Nevertheless, the models that address this problem are often modeled as one-dimensional knapsack, and thus it was necessary to model the capacity of AM machines through nesting issues, which lead to the development of the integrated nestingscheduling problem.
3.2.4. Integrated nesting-scheduling problem Although the integration of nesting and scheduling problems has
been only recently addressed, it remains of utmost importance to model the interdependence between these decisions. Following the research by Chergui et al. (2018), which is a seminal paper for integrated nesting and scheduling (see section 3.2.1), the comprehensive review of Oh et al. (2020) holds a pivotal position in our main path because it was the first review paper that has thoroughly combined both nesting and scheduling papers in a holistic manner. The authors proposed a taxonomy to classify nesting and scheduling problems into four classes depending on Part; Build, and AM Machine. In the M/S/S class, problems consider multiple parts grouped into a single build and assigned to a single machine. M/ M/S class covers problems where a single AM machine sequentially processes multiple builds, whereas in the M/M/M class a set of identical or non-identical machines are assigned to builds. Finally, if the optimi­ zation problem considers that only one part is assigned to each machine for a build cycle, these are classified into -/M/M class. Hence, by comprehensively covering nesting and scheduling problems in AM, the developed taxonomy complements the literature with a hierarchical perspective.
Faced with the hardness of solving both nesting and scheduling problems, Alicastro et al. (2021) used a Q-learning variable neighbor­ hood search to develop a Reinforcement Learning Iterated Local Search metaheuristic. The nesting problem discussed in their paper is called a strip-packing problem, which can be described through a rectangular bin with finite width and infinite height, in which the objective of this problem is to minimize the nesting height. In pursuit of simplicity, the authors considered bounding boxes to represent the parts. Thus, the problem is solved through a level-oriented method where parts are ar­ ranged in rows forming levels, such that in each level the set of parts are bottom-aligned. For the scheduling problem, non-identical SLM ma­ chines are considered to minimize the makespan.
Rohaninejad et al. (2022) addressed a non-identical SLM machines environment. Differing from the earlier efforts, this study did not investigate single-material part production, but rather SLM machines capable of working with different powder types, including steel, iron-, aluminum-, and titanium-based alloys. Hence, the parts belonging to a particular job must be of the same material type, while different material types can be used for successive builds. To produce a sequence of builds

8

M. Pinto et al.
with different material types, it is necessary to clean the SLM machine as well as to replace the related powder. This setup process requires additional time, and the setup time is sequence-dependent. Accordingly, a bi-objective MILP model was proposed considering the makespan and the total tardiness cost, and a hybrid metaheuristic was developed combining the Non-Dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm (NSGA-II) with a learning-based local search founded on a k-means clustering al­ gorithm and a regression neural network.
While previous papers have been broadening the efforts to encom­ pass mainly economic goals, Tafakkori et al. (2022) have been at the forefront of the research addressing sustainability concerns in AM. In their study, a multi-objective model was developed on the basis of the triple bottom line that includes profit, energy utilization of machines, and goodwill losses as the three sustainability criteria. Beyond part placement, the part orientation problem along with irregular geometries were considered into the nesting problem, and a non-identical parallel machine scheduling was solved. An order acceptance problem, in which the manufacturer has set some negotiation strategy to win orders based on increasing due date desired by customers for price reductions, were also considered. The model was solved by three algorithms: robust improved ε-constraint method, NSGA-II, and the multi-objective meta­ heuristic Grey Wolf Optimizer.
3.3. Critical remarks on the main path analysis
Although the main path analysis of nesting and scheduling in AM

Computers and Operations Research 165 (2024) 106592
provides a thorough view about the influence of each specific publica­ tion (Zupic and Cˇater, 2015), it is crucial to recognize that the AM field’s relative youth and rapid pace of progress may not be adequately captured in the citation network. To address this shortcoming, we included in the main path a secondary path analysis. The secondary paths point to pertinent research that ultimately contributes to draft a complete research territory. To conduct this analysis, we examined each pair of interconnected papers to uncover secondary streams gaining relevance within the nesting and scheduling domain. Fig. 8 shows two other paths - processing operations and collaborative manufacturing alongside the main path presented above.
3.3.1. Collaborative manufacturing This research stream introduces the collaborative multi-site pro­
duction problem in AM, wherein orders must be batched and scheduled at several geographically dispersed manufacturing sites. Given the unique characteristics of AM, this problem is particularly relevant. One of the strengths of AM is that apart from being a toolless production technologies, the entire production process is handled by a machinereadable format, i.e., each phase of the manufacturing process is digi­ tally captured and can be readily interpreted by machines or computer systems. These advantages allow the digital transfer of production batches from one machine to another, without the need for costly transfer methods (Zehetner and Gansterer, 2022). Therefore, firms could easily exchange orders among manufacturing plants to balance the use of facilities or even save transportation costs, as one manufacturing site

