may be situated closer to customers. Zehetner and Gansterer (2022) were the first to introduce the
collaborative multi-site production problem in AM. They developed a two-step approach, wherein the overall problem is decomposed into a nesting and a scheduling problem. Two different Genetic Algorithms were proposed for the nesting problem and a MILP model for the scheduling problem. Their following work (Zehetner and Gansterer, 2023) studies the relationship between collaborative production and Cloud Manufacturing (CMfg), since CMfg is considered an essential enabler for achieving collaborative production goals. The research conducted by Zehetner and Gansterer (2023) was influenced by the work of Zhou et al. (2018) and Liu et al. (2021). Zhou et al. (2018) focused their study on the supply–demand matching problem for AM services in CMfg. Different service attributes were considered, including model size, material, accuracy, cost, time, and transportation. To minimize the delivery time from service providers to customers, a Ge­ netic Algorithm was proposed. Likewise, Liu et al. (2021) developed a non-cooperative game model of AM services in a CMfg. The objective is to reduce the makespan, cost and to maximize the service quality.
CMfg can be seen as a technology-driven approach that uses cloud computing and networking technologies to facilitate the sharing of manufacturing resources, information, and capabilities over a network (Zhou et al., 2018). It acts as a technological infrastructure that supports efficient management and operation of the manufacturing process. AM can serve as a catalyst and demonstration of CMfg applications (Liu et al., 2021). At the same time, CMfg is a key technology to leverage AM systems, connecting suppliers to customers and to the CMfg operator, who is responsible for managing and operating the CMfg platform (Wang et al., 2019). Although collaborative manufacturing is a complex decision problem, for the convenience of AM research, the scheduling problem related to CMfg has been studied previously (Liu et al., 2021). Hence, the introduction of AM within CMfg is a sound direction to further improve the utilization of idle resources and achieve cost re­ ductions (Wu et al., 2022).
Recently, there has also been a proliferation of research on collab­ orative multi-robot manufacturing (Jin et al., 2019; Poudel et al., 2020; McPherson and Zhou, 2018; Poudel et al., 2021; Shen et al., 2019; Jiang et al., 2021; Weber et al., 2023). These studies suggest a new approach to provide scalability to AM, allowing multiple robots to cooperate on a single printing job (Shen et al., 2019). A large part is first divided into multiple chunks (divisions), which are then assigned to multiple robots to work cooperatively (Poudel et al., 2021). In this scenario, typical decisions include geometric partition of the large part into chunks, scheduling of robots to chunks, path planning (collision-free moving path planning for robots to transition from one workstation to another) and motion planning (collision-free infill paths generation for neigh­ boring robots to work together during manufacturing).
3.3.2. Processing operations Pre- and post-processing operations have a substantial impact on the
AM scheduling problem, namely when the parts have due dates and fixed delivery times. Aloui and Hadj-Hamou (2021) introduced two models for Power-based Laser and Multi Jet Fusion (MJF) technologies to estimate the production time, including file and machine preparation, layer construction, and post-processing operations (finishing, sorting and packaging). The objective is to reduce the total delay of the parts to be produced and to maximize the use rate of the AM machines. The authors solved the problem with an exact method using Mixed-Linear Programming and a heuristic procedure based on the Earliest Due Date (EDD) rule and the shortest estimated manufacturing time.
Meanwhile, Kapadia et al. (2022) considered the problem of order acceptance and scheduling, satisfying all technological constraints, including part placement and part orientation into the AM machine and post-processing operations for support removal and surface finishing. The authors used a Genetic Algorithm to select orders for complete or partial acceptance and to produce machine schedules such that the

Computers and Operations Research 165 (2024) 106592
profit is maximized. Specifically, post-processing operations pose sig­ nificant challenges for production scheduling since routings and pro­ cessing requirements vary, and therefore parts produced at AM machines often require alternative post-processing operations and in different sequences. Thürer et al. (2021) considered the use of workload control in a flow-shop where nine distinct pool sequencing rules aimed at minimizing shop floor throughput times and preventing starvation. Once a job is released and enters the queue at the AM station, three dispatching rules prioritize jobs awaiting processing in the queue of the station.
The work carried out in this stream suggests that explicit consider­ ation of pre- and post-processing operations can significantly improve the performance of AM scheduling, while also approaching practical needs. Particularly, the delivery time seems to be largely determined by post-processing stations, which may significantly constrain the effec­ tiveness of AM (Thürer et al., 2021). These stations, cause difficulties for production planning and, as a result, problems in adhering to promised due dates.
4. Model characteristics
To answer our second research question, we review the main char­ acteristics of the mathematical formulations for nesting and scheduling problems in AM. Model variants can be attributed to the different levels of detail and industrial contexts under consideration. Our analysis was centered on seven fundamental model aspects. Part placement and part orientation decisions were brought to the forefront of the discussion, since both have a great level of influence on the nesting problem. Given the different machine configurations addressed in the literature, we adopt a categorization considering single-machine, identical parallel machines, non-identical parallel machines, flow-shop, and job-shop. Machine allocation and sequencing decisions were considered main features of the scheduling problem. Finally, optimization issues are detailed through the assumptions or constraints, the objective function, and the solution approach used in the models. Table 2 shows the various assumptions used in the literature. The models were categorized based on the problems of nesting (N), scheduling (S), part decomposition (D), part distribution (T), and order acceptance (OA). Table 3 classifies the studies through the above-mentioned aspects.
