has been studied with the primary objective of maximizing the compactness of the build. The outcome is evident; by optimizing the compactness more parts within the build are accommodated and more parts are produced during one cycle time (Kucukkoc, 2021). Yet, the pressing question that emerges when considering compactness is how to model overlapping requirements between parts. While some authors model this issue by geometric approximations to represent the real form of the parts, thereby introducing a tolerance between the parts (Fera et al., 2018; Demir et al., 2018; Zehetner and Gansterer, 2022), other authors consider overlap concerns in the objective function (Che et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2022).
Even considering simplifications, such as geometric approximations, the optimization problem is not trivial, and the computational burden increases sharply with the irregularity of the geometries, and hence the model solution time also increases (Araújo et al., 2020). To overcome this situation, it is common practice to simplify the parts to regular ge­ ometries, including bounding boxes (Oh et al., 2018), cylinders (Zhang et al., 2002), and spheres (Dickinson and Knopf, 2002). Simplifications, while theoretically useful to reduce the computational burden, are far from practical for real-world scenarios. This has been noticed by scholars who have effectively solved the nesting problem involving irregular geometries. For a thorough and comprehensive understanding of nesting problems with irregular geometries, refer to the noteworthy review of Araújo et al. (2019).
To a lesser extent, the second most often discussed objective, the build height, directly impacts the production time and cost (Yau and Hsu, 2022). This occurs since AM machines operate layer-by-layer, and as the number of layers increases, the build time also grows. Given this premise, some authors consider that the build time corresponds to the production time of the highest part within the build (Edelkamp and Wichern, 2015). Other nesting objectives were considered on an ad hoc basis, including the minimization of the part’s distance within the build and the distance between parts and the build boundaries (Ikonen and Biles, 1997).
Regarding placement decisions, two alternative strategies have been discussed in the literature: parallel placement and serial placement. In

the parallel placement, all parts are positioned at once, and therefore, it is evident that parallel placements are usually solved by metaheuristics, including Simulated Annealing or Genetic Algorithms. Otherwise, serial placement decisions are essentially rule-based approaches, making them computationally less demanding, which often leads to their resolution using heuristics.
Ensuring the proper orientation of parts within the build is another critical aspect of modeling. According to the literature, there are two different strategies: two-step and integrated. In the two-step approach, orientations are determined first and then the placement of parts is decided already with the orientations fixed from the preceding phase (Cao et al., 2015). In some models, the first step considers rotations on Xand Y-axes for build orientation, and then in the following step, rotations on the Z-axis (Gogate and Pande, 2008; Che et al., 2021; Li et al., 2019; Aloui and Hadj-Hamou, 2021). In general, the two-step approach pro­ vides lower computational complexity than the integrated approach. In the integrated method, the above-mentioned first and second steps are iteratively or simultaneously used to reach an optimized solution. It should be noted that modeling the orientation of parts elevates the nesting problem to the category of NP-hard (Li and Zhang, 2018), which leads some authors to adopt a pre-determined part orientation, and therefore reducing the orientation aspect to a single rotation along an axis (Shen et al., 2019; Jiang et al., 2021; Garashchenko et al., 2022).
In scheduling problems, the primary objective is the minimization of makespan, followed by the minimization of tardiness. In this sense, nonidentical parallel machines were well-addressed by the literature, fol­ lowed by identical parallel machines and then single-machine configu­ rations. Slightly less considered were configurations with upstream and downstream operations (Li et al., 2022; Canellidis et al., 2006). This means that pre-processing and post-processing operations, which are integral stages for the majority of AM systems (Thürer et al., 2021), have been widely neglected when developing scheduling models. It is a novel research stream identified by our main path analysis.
In multi-material AM scenarios, where various materials may need to be used in a single build or production cycle, including setup times be­ comes essential to address practical scenarios. When solving the scheduling problem, some authors considered sequence-dependent setup times (Rohaninejad et al., 2021). However, this requirement

