needs to be further studied in AM. Another modeling characteristic is the allocation of orders from
different customer in an CMfg environment. This scenario is gaining popularity in the AM scheduling problem. Likewise, hybrid manufacturing appears to be a likely scenario in the foreseeable future given the coexistence of AM and traditional processes. However, only a restricted number of studies model the scheduling problem based on the conditions of coexistence, such as Flow-shop and Job-shop configurations.
In terms of used approaches, nesting problems are often addressed using metaheuristic algorithms, being the Genetic Algorithm the pre­ dominant technique, followed by Simulated Annealing. Rule-based heuristics were frequently incorporated in these metaheuristics, for example, both Bottom-Left (Hur et al., 2001) and Left Border-Down Border algorithms (Canellidis et al., 2013) are widely used for serial part placement. Exact algorithms were considered to a lesser extent in the domain of nesting.
In the context of scheduling problems, heuristic and metaheuristics methods continue to stand out as the most often used algorithms. Yet, there is an increased application of exact algorithms, including MixedInteger Programming (Griffiths et al., 2019; Jiang et al., 2021; Weber et al., 2023) and MILP (Rohaninejad et al., 2022; Hur et al., 2000; Zhang et al., 2002) models. It is important to acknowledge that exact algo­ rithms still have limited practicality when dealing with large-scale optimization problems, like those encountered in industrial-sized scheduling challenges.
Finally, integrated nesting-scheduling models were usually solved by metaheuristic algorithms (Arık, 2022; Zehetner and Gansterer, 2023; Dickinson and Knopf, 2002) due to their complexity. Nevertheless, in recent developments, hybrid algorithms that blend metaheuristics with machine learning techniques have emerged as prominent directions of research to solve the integrated nesting-scheduling problem.
5. Future research avenues
The nesting and scheduling problem has progressed significantly. But there remain relevant issues in this field that still require further attention. The most pressing research avenues to further advance the field are outlined in the following sub sections.
5.1. Closing the distance between modeling and reality
Assumptions specify the conditions under which a mathematical model is expected to be accurate and relevant for decision-support. Yet, relaxed assumptions, in some cases, become necessary to address more complex real-world situations and to strike a balance between model simplicity and utility. In contrast, tightening the models is a natural move to elevate the quality of solutions.
An important relaxation issue is associated with the complexity of parts in the nesting models. There is an expectation for a future increase in the adoption of real-world geometries, rather than oversimplified regular geometries, including bounding boxes. This oversimplification may result in suboptimal nesting solutions, impacting material utiliza­ tion and production efficiency. Future research should include the ability to handle irregular geometries more effectively, allowing for a more realistic representation of each part. Nonetheless, this stresses the importance of the model also incorporating advanced non-overlapping constraints, since the interaction between irregular geometries poses a significant modelling challenge.
Incorporating uncertainties is also essential for delivering robust solutions. For example, uncertainty in demand, parts’ characteristics, arrival times, and availability of machines. Some quality issues may also be considered, for example, parts being damaged that need to be pro­ duced again.

