5.2. Solving interrelated problems
One of those sound integrations is the consideration of AM sched­ uling with the distribution of parts to the final customer. The scarcity of research here indicates that while the integration of nesting and scheduling problems has witnessed substantial attention, the extension to include distribution of parts is an offshoot where further in­ vestigations are warranted.
Another key integration that has unfolded, though with subtle expression, is the consideration of part decomposition concerns with nesting. Given the interrelated nature of these decisions that have been typically addressed separately, there is ample room for integration. Going a little further, it would be interesting to extend the decomposition-and-nesting problem to include scheduling consider­ ations. For example, when a part is divided into smaller parts and grouped into builds, these parts are typically manufactured on a single machine. Nonetheless, an extension of this problem would involve manufacturing the parts into different builds that are then distributed across parallel AM machines.
Other decisions can be further considered with nesting and sched­ uling problems in AM. One of the less frequent but important ones, is the integration of inventory issues, including supply chain management and spare parts management. AM features should be distinguished from traditional inventory topics. In traditional manufacturing, inventory management often focuses on maintaining stock levels of finished goods, raw materials, and components. Yet, in AM the dynamic nature of pro­ duction and on-demand manufacturing necessitate a reevaluation of inventory strategies. Integrating inventory decisions into the nesting and scheduling problems in AM facilitates a more streamlined process.
5.3. Modeling emerging AM technologies and scenarios
CMfg and mobile printing robots seem to be two key technologies for leveraging the AM system and vice versa. CMfg is a sound avenue to further improve the utilization of idle AM machines and achieve cost reductions based on collaborative production models. Although prom­ ising, CMfg has only recently been brought into the discussion of AM scheduling problems, and thus, there is still ample room for further research. For example, the consideration of factors such as dynamic arrival of orders, acceptance of orders, downtime of AM machines, and capabilities of logistic services are all needed to further advance CMfg AM scheduling.
Collaborative multi-robot manufacturing suggests a new paradigm to provide scalability to AM, but as for CMfg AM scheduling research, also the research on AM multi-robot scheduling is still in its infancy. A major challenge is to delineate multiple chunks within complex shapes. In this domain, different avenues to represent the chunks could be considered. Furthermore, existing works utilize a limited number of robots. Future research should focus on the application of more robots for cooperative AM, promoting higher utilization of the available robots.
Another technological improvement in AM systems is multi-material technologies, such as the multi-material SLA machines, which can pro­ duce a single part using different materials. This technique could simplify certain decisions, including the scheduling of parts from different materials in the same build. A paper exploring this technology is Kim et al. (2010). Given the surging demand for orders requiring multiple materials, multi-material technologies can be used for diverse applications that can take advantage of not only SLA but many other AM technologies. Investigations could also be extended to specialized AM machines supporting multi-laser, multi-extruder, and multi-axis. Furthermore, advanced AM machines that are capable of a multi-step process can reduce the complexity of production scheduling.
5.4. Integrating AM into existing processes

It is foreseeable that AM and traditional processes will coexist.

13

M. Pinto et al.
Nevertheless, only few works have modelled the AM scheduling problem based on these conditions (Makanda et al., 2022; Kapadia et al., 2022; Rossi and Lanzetta, 2020; Freens et al., 2015). In a coexistence scenario, the decision-maker has the flexibility to select between AM machines and traditional manufacturing machines for producing a set of specific parts. This decision can prove advantageous for planning purposes, considering that AM is characterized by reduced throughput and rela­ tively high costs, even though the specific cost is contingent on factors including material choices and build volume (Maranha et al., 2023). However, in opposition to traditional manufacturing machines, AM enables the production of geometrically complex parts and offers flexi­ bility for product design (Maranha et al., 2023). This scenario provides the opportunity to adapt production scheduling, particularly for dy­ namic orders.
Even when all parts are manufactured through AM, there is evidence suggesting that, specifically, post-processing stations are required to ensure surface quality, support removal, and dimensional accuracy. However, most AM scheduling studies are based on single and parallel machine settings. This suggests an opportunity to extend the research horizon to incorporate flow-shop and job-shop models. Rework and repair operations can also be included as downstream AM options. Indeed, post-processing operations poses significant challenges for production scheduling and control since routings and processing re­ quirements vary. This may result in unbalanced workloads at down­ stream AM stations. Future research could explore optimization-based workload control. Integrating post-processing operations with workload control and order acceptance decisions seems to be another interesting research stream.
5.5. Adopting new solution approaches
Our analysis demonstrated that hybrid algorithms that blend meta­ heuristics with machine learning techniques represent the most recent advancements in solution approaches. More research is required to further advance this promising field. Meanwhile, machine learning is widely used to recognize patterns, which is commonly referred to as computer vision. In AM, for part orientation decisions, machine learning could analyze the characteristics of 3D CAD models and determine the optimal orientation for each part. This approach can minimize support structures, reduce build time, and improve the quality of parts. Addi­ tionally, for part placement, machine learning can autonomously arrange parts on the build. Moreover, a distinct aspect is that these al­ gorithms can learn from historical data and adapt to different shapes and product requirements.
Another opportunity lies in the application of machine learning to dynamic manufacturing, based on real-time factors, including machine and material availability, and machine downtimes. Additionally, these models can be trained to predict the quality of parts, considering various parameters, including machine settings, material characteristics, and environmental conditions. Another potential venue is the use of machine learning to optimize AM supply chain processes, including inventory management and logistics.
5.6. Achieving environmental sustainability
A paramount concern pervading several manufacturing systems todate relates to the environment and the sustainability of operations. By examining various aspects of AM, from material selection to printing techniques, researchers have identified opportunities to minimize the carbon footprint of AM operations, specifically studying the reduction of energy consumption and cost through nesting and scheduling or adjusting of process parameters. For example, Karimi et al. (2021) conducted a first study addressing energy issues while scheduling AM machines, while Tafakkori et al. (2022) and Lin et al. (2023) focused on reducing energy consumption by better utilizing the build space of machines and scheduling. Rupp et al. (2021) and Kucukkoc (2023) were

