among the first to address the AM machine scheduling considering emissions caused by production and transportation activities.
Despite these promising first steps, future research is needed that expands and deepens the investigation of carbon emissions in AM. This includes modeling low-emission strategies in which nesting and sched­ uling decisions benefit, for example, from better material usage, lower energy consumption, and more sustainable supply chains. In particular, additional research work is required to reduce material waste (some AM processes may generate more waste material than others) and remanu­ facturing possibilities (this could involve fixing damaged parts or mak­ ing design changes to enhance the durability or functionality of parts). Regarding energy consumption, more work can be done to improve energy efficiency and reduce the energy bill in AM. Incorporating renewable energy sources is a new challenge that may require consid­ erations of the intermittency and variability of renewable energy gen­ eration and how it impacts the overall energy consumption profile of AM technologies. Finally, future work should address the carbon emissions associated with production and transportation in AM supply chains. This involves production scheduling decisions aimed at reducing peak power demand and shift electricity usage in response to time-varying rates, along with optimizing transportation modes and distance from raw materials to manufacturing and from manufacturing to the customer.
6. Conclusion
This paper set out to summarize the challenges posed by AM in the field of operational research. Science mapping techniques and tradi­ tional systematic review methods were used to capture the progression of knowledge on nesting and scheduling models in the context of AM. One major critique on the proposed models is that most of them still lag behind the practical needs of real manufacturing systems. In the midst of this issue, industries are demanding solutions to smoothly integrate AM processes into their supply chains and manufacturing systems.
As main findings of our study, the nesting and scheduling problems can be classified into six decision types. Modeling the interdependencies between all these decisions poses a complex, yet important problems for research. Although we notice that there are various assumptions that restrict existing models, we argue that nesting and scheduling research in AM has experienced notable advancement in recent years. Our find­ ings demonstrate that this progression is supported by sound research streams that include new perspectives and functional integrations to the primary problem. In short, we identified relevant research problems: part decomposition, part distribution, order acceptance and scheduling, processing operations, collaborative manufacturing, and multi-robot manufacturing to be addressed together with the nesting and sched­ uling problem.
